summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Follow-up for adult hydrocephalus.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Mr. Christopher Watson is an 86-year-old male who returns for a follow-up visit for adult hydrocephalus. The patient was initially seen in 05/2020 for progressive gait impairment, cognitive impairment, and decreased bladder control. The patient underwent a spino catheter protocol in 06/2020 and subsequently underwent shunt surgery on 08/01/2020. A Medtronic Strata Programmable Shunt in the ventricular peritoneal configuration was placed and programmed at a level 2.0.

The patient reports that he has not been hospitalized since surgery. The patient states that his walking has improved and he is now able to walk to the dining room. He states he still has times where he needs assistance. He notes that his bladder control has improved some after surgery but has leveled off. He is now wearing a diaper. He denies any sores from the diaper or decreased or painful urination. He denies any headaches or pain where the shunt is. He denies any pain in his belly. The patient states that he feels like he can have a normal conversation with someone now.

The patient's daughter reports that she has seen an improvement in her father's walking. She also states that he is able to have normal conversations with her now. She states that he is still having some issues with bladder control.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies impaired ability to carry out daily functions. Denies fever or unintentional weight loss.
Gastrointestinal: Denies decreased or painful urination. Denies any pain in his belly.
Genitourinary: Urinary incontinence and urgency.
Neurological: Denies headaches, recent falls, or hallucinations.
Psychiatric: Denies depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings.

VITALS

Blood pressure: 124/80
Heart rate: 64
Respirations: 18
Weight: 174 pounds
Pain: 0/10.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Neurological: Pleasant and cooperative. Cranial nerves: Pupils are equal. EOMs are intact. Face is symmetric. No dysarthria. Motor: Normal for bulk and strength. Coordination: Slow from no- to nose. Gait: Testing using Tinetti assessment tool: Gait score of 6 to 8/12. Balance score of 12/16. Total score of 18 to 20/28. Slightly improved from initial score of 15 to 17/28.

RESULTS

CT scan of the head from 10/15/2020 shows a frontal horn span at the level of foramen of Monro of 4.6 cm with a third ventricular contour that is flat with a span of 10 mm. There is a tiny amount of blood in the right front region with a tiny subdermal collection. This was not noticed or noted by the radiologist who stated any extracranial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.

ASSESSMENT

• Adult hydrocephalus.
• Gait impairment.
• Urinary incontinence and urgency.
• Cognitive impairment.

PLAN

- Continue to monitor patient's symptoms and follow-up in 3 months.
- I discussed with the patient that he may not be able to drive at this time due to his cognitive and gait impairment. I advised him that he could enroll in a driver safety program without my recommendation. I also advised that if he improves he could pass the driver safety evaluation at that time.
- I also discussed with the patient that he may need to have his shunt revised in the future. I will continue to monitor him and reevaluate in 3 months.
- I advised the patient to continue to use the cane for ambulation.
- I advised the patient to continue to use the catheter.
- All questions were answered.
- I will order the driver's evaluation and send to the patient.
- I will order the CT scan and send to the patient.
- The patient's daughter will make the appointment for the next visit.

INSTRUCTIONS

Follow-up in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>
