summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Hypertension.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Amanda Taylor is a 72-year-old female who presents for follow-up for hypertension. The patient's history is significant for alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia. At her last visit on 04/16/2021, her blood pressure was 130/90. Today, she presents at 150/100. She was prescribed hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg by mouth once daily and lisinopril 20 mg by mouth once daily. CMP and CBC were essentially unremarkable.

The patient reports she is struggling with not drinking. She wanted to discuss the shot that was mentioned at the last visit, but they did not get to discuss it too much.

The patient states she has been drinking on Monday. She reports drinking 4-5 times a week, hard alcohol. She had been drinking for a while. She is taking half of the lisinopril. She reports she felt lethargic and tired the first time she took it so she only took half of the dose the next time. She feels much better and she does not have the headaches or the "swoshing" that started in her left ear. She feels like it is definitely helping but she does not know it is not going to be okay until she stops drinking.

She is taking the full dose of the hydrochlorothiazide. She denies chest tightness, pressure, or shortness of breath. She is doing blood pressure checks at home. In the morning, she sees lower numbers than in the evening. She reports blood pressure readings ranging from 130-145 over 95-100 in the morning and 130-150 over 100 in the evening. She also reports that her blood pressure is always high when she comes to the clinic but then it goes down. She is taking her medications in the morning. She states it sounds like we are not too far out of where we want to be.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Denies fever, cough, shortness of breath, or chills.
• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain, pressure, or dyspnea on exertion.
• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.
• Neurological: Denies tremor.

PHYSICAL EXAMINATION

• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation, unlabored breathing.
• Cardiovascular: Regular rhythm, no murmurs, no gallop, JVP flat at 90 degrees.
• Musculoskeletal: No edema.

VITALS

• Blood pressure: 150/100

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

