The patient is doing well today. She is doing well with her blood pressure medications. She is currently drinking 4-5 times a week to excess. She is not taking the lisinopril as prescribed. She is taking the full dose of the hydrochlorothiazide. She denies chest pain, shortness of breath, or other cardiac symptoms.

The patient would like to start naltrexone. We previously discussed this. She will take the injectable form and start on 05/17/2021. We reviewed the side effects and risks. The potential benefit of alcohol cessation would be highly beneficial to her for many reasons. Hypertension, she is taking lisinopril 10 mg daily and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily. Quite possibly her blood pressure will improve completely with cessation of alcohol. Her target BP is 130-140/70-80. She will be seen back week of 06/14/2021, which is likely around the time of her second naltrexone injection. I recommended upgrading influenza vaccination and Shingrix. Number 2, she did defer this.

INSTRUCTIONS

Continue her current medications. She will follow up with me in June.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

