summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Ongoing management of rheumatoid arthritis.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Tyler Nelson is a pleasant 72-year-old male who presents today for ongoing management of rheumatoid arthritis. The patient was last seen in clinic in October 2020 for similar complaints. At that time, he received a right knee steroid injection. He reports that the injection provided relief for a couple of weeks. However, his right knee has been swollen since that time. The patient denies any pain at rest, but states that his pain level is about a 6 on a scale of 1 to 10 on a typical day. He reports that his pain level is currently a 3. The patient denies any limitation in his activities of daily living. However, he does state that he has slowed down and has found himself doing things too fast. When he has been sitting for a while, he reports that he is stiff getting up and often uses a cane to get his bearings. The patient notes that after a little bit of activity, his pain loosens up. The patient reports that he has been taking his medications regularly, including Xeljanz 11 milligrams daily and Celebrex 200 milligrams daily, which he started about 2 months ago.

The patient denies any recent trauma to the right knee. He denies any fever, chills, or constitutional symptoms. He denies any recent travel. He denies any sick contacts.

The patient reports that he is a salt lover and enjoys salty snacks.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Musculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain, swelling, and stiffness. Endorses decreased range of motion.
• Neurological: Denies numbness, tingling, or weakness.
• Constitutional: Denies fever, chills.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• Musculoskeletal: Examination of the right lower extremity demonstrates slightly diminished range of motion. Pitting around the knee and ankle. Normal capillary refill in the toes. The knee is hot to the touch.

RESULTS

X-rays of the right knee were obtained today and are unremarkable.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

1. Right knee inflammation.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents today with right knee pain, swelling, and stiffness. He received a right knee steroid injection in October 2020. He has been taking Xeljanz 11 milligrams daily and Celebrex 200 milligrams daily, which he started 2 months ago. He denies any recent trauma.
• Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the risks, benefits, and alternatives of fluid aspiration and steroid injection. The risks of steroid injection were explained, including but not limited to hypopigmentation, soft tissue atrophy, tendon ruptures, and transient hypoglycemia among others. Verbal consent was attained. The patient was advised to follow up with his primary care physician. We also discussed the importance of salt reduction and leg elevation, especially at night.
• Medical Treatment: Fluid aspiration and steroid injection were performed today. Right knee was injected with 80 milligrams of triamcinolone and 1 milliliter of lidocaine. The procedure was tolerated well, with no complications.
• Additional Testing: The patient will follow up with his primary care physician.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with his primary care physician. }}<|eot_id|>
