HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Jacqueline Miller is a pleasant 61-year-old female who presents for a follow-up today. She was last seen in clinic in 2 weeks ago. She reports that she is doing well and her rash is doing much better. She notes that she has some residual rash that she started using doxycycline once a day because her pharmacist told her to limit it to once a day. She notes that she was taking it twice a day and noticed improvement, but when she started taking it once a day it plateaued. She denies breastfeeding. The patient stopped breastfeeding and requested doxycycline at that time. She was hesitant to use Elidel because she was concerned about the side effects. The provider explained that the side effects she read about were for the oral form used after a heart or kidney transplant and not for as needed use of the cream or ointment. She notes that she stopped breastfeeding and started taking doxycycline. She is currently using the Sulfacetamide wash twice daily without dryness. She is using the Metro cream after her face wash. She denies using Cetaphil cleanser or Neutrogena wipes. She denies using any lotions. She is not using any other type of lotion. She was going to try Elidel, but insurance is not covering it. She checked and it is $850 with her coupon. She also looked at Eucrisa, which is also expensive.

