summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Abdominal pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Bobby Johnson is a pleasant 61-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of abdominal pain. The onset of the pain began approximately 3 weeks ago. The pain is located on the right side of the abdomen, just below the ribs. The pain is described as squeezing and severe in intensity. The patient notes that he becomes nauseated when the pain occurs. The patient rates the pain as an 8 out of 10 in severity. The pain can occur at any time, but it is most severe after meals. He has noticed that the pain is associated with fatty foods. He has been following a keto diet for the past 3 months and has lost 25 pounds. The patient denies vomiting but notes that he has had low-grade fevers. He denies any family history of gallbladder disease.

SOCIAL HISTORY

The patient reports that he is a photographer and enjoys nature photography.

MEDICATIONS

The patient reports that he is taking medications for his diabetes.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies fevers.
HENT: Denies chest pain, shortness of breath, or abdominal pain.
Gastrointestinal: Reports abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting.
Musculoskeletal: Denies joint pain.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Vital signs are normal. Blood pressure is 128/88 mmHg, respiratory rate is 16, pulse is 68, and oxygen saturation is 99% on room air. Auscultation of the heart reveals regular rate and rhythm, no ectopy, no clicks, or murmurs. Lungs are clear bilaterally. Auscultation of the abdomen reveals bowel sounds present but slightly diminished. The abdomen is soft and nontender but there is slight guarding to the right upper quadrant. There is some pain with palpation in that area. Murphy's sign is present but there is no rebound tenderness. No peritoneal signs.

RESULTS

Abdominal ultrasound demonstrates multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. There is also mild thickening of the gallbladder wall.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

1. Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.
* Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with abdominal pain that has been ongoing for approximately 3 weeks. His symptoms are associated with fatty foods. His abdominal ultrasound revealed multiple gallstones and mild thickening of the gallbladder wall.
* Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed the nature of his symptoms and the results of his abdominal ultrasound with the patient. I advised him that given his symptoms have not improved with dietary modifications, it is recommended that he undergo a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. I also advised him that he should avoid fatty foods after surgery and eat foods high in fiber. I recommended that he follow up with his primary care physician for his diabetes.
* Medical Treatment: We will schedule a laparoscopic cholecystectomy.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in 1 week for postoperative care. }}<|eot_id|>
