summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right shoulder pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Matthew Jackson is a pleasant 67-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of right shoulder pain. The onset of the pain began approximately 4 days ago when he was playing tackle football. He reports that he was tackled and landed on his right shoulder in an awkward manner. He states that he was able to continue playing, but has been experiencing pain in his right shoulder since the incident. He denies any numbness or tingling in his fingers. He denies any problems with the strength of his arm. He is right-handed and denies any problems with his left arm. He reports that he can grab a cup of coffee, but notes that he is avoiding lifting heavy objects. He rates his pain as 10/10. He has tried taking Tylenol, but it has not provided any relief.

The patient denies any neck problems and denies any other joint pain. He reports that he has been experiencing a slight headache but denies any fever. He denies any loss of consciousness. He denies any other medical history except for a history of appendicitis and a subsequent appendectomy 3-4 years ago.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports right shoulder pain.
Neurological: Denies numbness or tingling in his fingers.
Neck: Denies neck pain.

VITALS

Blood pressure: 122/76 mmHg.
Temperature: No fever.
Pulse: 82 bpm.
Respiratory rate: 16 breaths per minute.
Oxygen saturation: 95% on room air.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Neurological
- Orientation: Alert and oriented x3.
- Cranial nerves: Normal.
- Motor strength: Normal strength throughout all extremities.
- Reflexes: Normal reflexes throughout all extremities.

Musculoskeletal
- Cervical spine: No tenderness to palpation. Full range of motion.
- Right shoulder: Pain to palpation over the posterior shoulder at the AC joint. Decreased abduction and adduction to 90 degrees. Pain with supination and pronation of the right arm. Palpable radial artery pulse.

RESULTS

X-ray images of the right shoulder were obtained in office today and reveal an acute AC joint fracture.

ASSESSMENT

Right shoulder pain.
Acute AC joint fracture.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have recommended that we place him in a sling for now. I have prescribed Meloxicam 15 mg once a day. We will order a CT scan of the right shoulder to get a better look at the joint. We will refer him to physical therapy to strengthen his shoulder in hopes of avoiding surgery in the future. We discussed the possibility of surgery, but I do not think that is necessary at this time.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in 1 week. }}<|eot_id|>
