summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Shortness of breath.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Dennis Brown is a 57-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for shortness of breath. He has a history of diabetes, hypertension, and asthma. The onset of his shortness of breath began approximately 2 weeks ago. The patient describes waking up with shortness of breath. He states he is short of breath when he is at rest and also when he exerts himself. He also complains of hemoptysis, stating he is coughing up approximately 3 to 4 cups per day. He denies any hematemesis. He denies any bleeding from his teeth or gums. He denies any leg swelling. He denies any recent travel history. He denies any fevers. The patient was unable to get an appointment in the emergency department.

He is currently taking Norvasc daily. He states he has been taking Norvasc but sometimes skips doses because his blood pressure has been running somewhat low. He denies any recent hospital admissions.

The patient is a diabetic and has been on metformin 500 mg twice daily. He has been wearing a Dexcom. He states that his blood sugars have been running high, stating that they spike up to over 200 after meals. He also states that his morning blood sugars are around 150. He denies any recent changes in his diet or exercise.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Negative for fevers.
• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or dyspnea on exertion.
• Respiratory: Positive for shortness of breath and hemoptysis.
• Musculoskeletal: Positive for lower extremity swelling.

VITALS

• Oxygen saturation is 94% on pulse oximetry.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• Neck: No JVD. No carotid bruits.
• Respiratory: Slightly diminished breath sounds on the right. Some rhonchi on the right.
• Cardiovascular: 2/6 holosystolic ejection murmur. Otherwise regular rate and rhythm.
• Musculoskeletal: 1+ nonpitting edema in both lower extremities. No calf tenderness. No Homan's sign.

RESULTS

Hemoglobin A1c is 7.0.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

1. Shortness of breath.
* Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with shortness of breath and hemoptysis. He has a history of diabetes, hypertension, and asthma. His recent hemoglobin A1c was 7.0. I heard diminished breath sounds on the right with rhonchi. He has 1+ edema in both lower extremities.
* Additional Testing: I am going to refer the patient to the emergency department for a chest x-ray and a CT chest. I also want to get some blood testing done.
* Patient Education and Counseling: I explained the nature of his symptoms to the patient. I advised him that he may need to start antibiotics or other treatment for his shortness of breath.
* Medical Treatment: I advised him to hold off on taking Norvasc for now.

2. Diabetes.
* Medical Reasoning: The patient is a diabetic who has been on metformin 500 mg twice daily. He has been wearing a Dexcom. He reports that his blood sugars have been running high.
* Additional Testing: I will order another hemoglobin A1c today.
* Medical Treatment: I advised him to increase his metformin to 750 mg twice daily. I advised him to continue wearing the Dexcom. I told him to come back in 3 weeks to discuss his diabetes.

3. Hypertension.
* Medical Reasoning: The patient has hypertension. His blood pressure is currently 120/70 mmHg.
* Medical Treatment: I advised him to hold off on taking Norvasc for now. I told him to come back once his shortness of breath is resolved.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.
