summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right knee pain and swelling.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Scott Davis is a pleasant 34-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right knee pain and swelling. The onset of his pain began 3 days ago when he attempted to go out and play soccer. He describes feeling a pop in his right knee when he attempted to kick the ball. He notes that the swelling has decreased slightly. He also notes that his knee does not function as well as it used to. He states that it seems limited. The patient rates his pain as 6 to 7 out of 10. He states that ibuprofen has helped a little bit. He rates his pain as 4 to 5 out of 10 with some of the swelling decreasing. He notes that he can bend his knee, but it hurts at the end of the motion. He can straighten his knee, but it does not feel normal. He states that he can still bear weight on his right leg, but it feels loose and does not feel normal.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain.
Neurological: Denies any numbness or tingling in the right leg.
Skin: Denies any rash or redness in the right knee.

PHYSICAL EXAM

MSK
- Gait: Marked limp on the right.
- Examination of the right knee: Slight effusion. Ecchymosis. Pain to palpation over the medial joint line. Positive Lachman test.

RESULTS

X-rays of the right knee were obtained and are unremarkable.

ASSESSMENT

Right knee sprain.
Right knee strain.
Right ACL tear, suspected.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regard to his current symptoms. I have recommended that we obtain an MRI of the right knee to further evaluate the integrity of the ACL. I have also recommended that we start him on meloxicam 50 mg daily for pain and swelling. I have also recommended that he use ice as needed. I have also advised him to limit his activities and to use crutches when ambulating. I have advised him to continue to work but to avoid jogging for the time being.

Patient's Question
Scott Davis: "Do I have to limit my work activities?"

Doctor's Answer
Yes, I know you sit at a desk most of the time, but you need to move your leg every now and then so it does not lock up. I would like you to limit your activities for the time being.

Patient's Question
Scott Davis: "What about my dog?"

Doctor's Answer
I'm glad to hear that your dog is doing well. You will not be able to jog with him for a while, but you can continue to play with him outside.

Patient's Question
Scott Davis: "What about my job?"

Doctor's Answer
Yes, you can continue to work, but you will need to limit your activities and use crutches when walking. }}<|eot_id|>
