summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right elbow pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Teresa Rodriguez is a pleasant 63-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of right elbow pain. The onset of her pain began 4 days ago after she has been working on a pottery wheel. She describes the pain as medial and radiating down her arm. She denies any direct trauma to the area. She rates her pain as a 7 out of 10. The patient reports that the pain wakes her up at night. She has tried ibuprofen with minimal relief. She denies any numbness, tingling, or weakness in her arm.

The patient reports that she has been experiencing hypertension for a while now. She has been compliant with taking her lisinopril 10 mg daily. She also reports that she has been compliant with taking her metformin 500 mg twice daily for her diabetes. She states that she has been checking her blood sugars regularly and reports that they have been in the 110s to 130s. She has been having a sweet tooth lately and has been baking and making cakes and cinnamon rolls for parties. She also enjoys making pottery. She reports that she has been doing her home exercise program and taking her blood pressure medication daily.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.
Neurological: Denies numbness and tingling.
Cardiovascular: Reports hypertension.

VITALS

Blood pressure: 120/70 mmHg.
Respiratory rate: 18 breaths per minute.
Temperature: 97.9 F.
Oxygen saturation: 100% on room air.
Pulse: 72 beats per minute.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Respiratory: Lungs are clear bilaterally to auscultation.
Cardiovascular: No murmur, no gallop.
Neurological: No focal neurological deficits.
Musculoskeletal: Examination of the right elbow: Moderate tenderness over the medial epicondyle. No pain with right elbow extension or flexion. Pain with resisted pronation of the right forearm. No pain with right elbow rotation. No ecchymosis, no swelling.

RESULTS

X-ray of the right elbow: Normal.

ASSESSMENT

1. Right elbow pain.
2. Diabetes.
3. Hypertension.

PLAN

Right elbow pain.
Medical Reasoning: This is consistent with medial epicondylitis, which is pain caused by overuse and damage to the tendons in the arm.

Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed my diagnosis and treatment plan in detail today. I advised the patient to rest her right arm and to apply ice to the elbow for 20 minutes 3 times a day. I also advised her to take 600 mg of ibuprofen every 6 hours with food. I advised her to hold off on pottery for the next couple of weeks. I encouraged her to take pictures of her pottery project so she can show it to her son.

Medical Treatment: I have prescribed ibuprofen 600 mg every 6 hours with food. I have also provided a sling for her to wear while she is awake.

Right elbow pain.
Medical Reasoning: This is consistent with medial epicondylitis, which is pain caused by overuse and damage to the tendons in the arm.

Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed my diagnosis and treatment plan in detail today. I advised the patient to rest her right arm and to apply ice to the elbow for 20 minutes 3 times a day. I also advised her to take 600 mg of ibuprofen every 6 hours with food. I advised her to hold off on pottery for the next couple of weeks. I encouraged her to take pictures of her pottery project so she can show it to her son.

Medical Treatment: I have prescribed ibuprofen 600 mg every 6 hours with food.

Diabetes.
Medical Reasoning: She has been noncompliant with her metformin 500 mg twice daily.

Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to take her metformin 1000 mg twice daily. I also advised her to check her blood sugars daily.

Medical Treatment: I have increased her metformin to 1000 mg twice daily.

Hypertension.
Medical Reasoning: Her blood pressure is well-controlled on lisinopril 10 mg daily.

Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to continue taking her lisinopril 10 mg daily.

Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 10 mg daily.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>
