Back pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Noah Rodriguez is a pleasant 65-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of low back pain. The onset of the pain began 1 week ago. He denies any injury to the back. He states that the pain has been continuous since the onset and has not resolved with over-the-counter medications. The pain is localized to the right and middle low back and radiates to the left. The pain is aggravated by sudden movements to the left. He denies numbness or tingling in his feet.

The patient has a history of diabetes. He is currently taking metformin twice daily. He denies any recent A1c tests. He states he tries to exercise and watch his diet. He is currently taking Tylenol with ibuprofen as needed for pain.

The patient denies any family history of diabetes.

MEDICAL HISTORY

The patient has a history of diabetes.

SURGICAL HISTORY

The patient has no history of surgery.

SOCIAL HISTORY

The patient reports that he works as an outdoorsman. He enjoys skiing and snowboarding.

FAMILY HISTORY

The patient has a family history of diabetes.

MEDICATIONS

Patient reports taking ibuprofen and Tylenol as needed for pain. He is currently taking metformin twice daily.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports low back pain.
Neurological: Denies numbness or tingling in his feet.

VITALS

