summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right ankle pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Gregory Martin is a pleasant 57-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of right ankle pain. The onset of his pain began yesterday after he fell on ice. He was walking to take out the trash when he slipped on a patch of ice. He states that he was being careful but slipped and fell to the ground. He was with a friend who assisted him up. He denies any numbness in the foot.

The patient reports that he could not bear weight on his right ankle initially. He states that he was able to rest, ice, and elevate the right ankle last night. Today, he states that he is able to put a little weight on the right ankle but he is still limping. He reports that his pain has improved but he is still experiencing some pain. He has been taking ibuprofen, which he states has helped.

The patient reports that he is an avid soccer player and has been playing in the community league. He denies any previous injuries to this right ankle.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports right ankle pain.
Neurological: Denies numbness in the right foot.

VITALS

All vital signs are within normal limits.

PHYSICAL EXAM

CV: Capillary refill is less than 3 seconds in the right foot. Strong dorsalis pedis pulse.
NEURO: Normal sensation to light touch in the right foot.
MSK: Examination of the right ankle: Ecchymosis over the lateral malleolus. Associated swelling. Tenderness to palpation anterolaterally. No laxity on anterior drawer and inversion stress. No bony tenderness on palpation of the foot.

RESULTS

X-rays of the right ankle were obtained and reviewed today. There is no evidence of fracture.

ASSESSMENT

Right ankle sprain.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have recommended that he continue to elevate his right leg, especially when seated, and to continue to ice the right ankle. I have also recommended that he wear an Aircast to help stabilize the ankle. He will also be given crutches for the next 1-2 days. He may start walking on it as tolerated. I have also recommended that he take NSAIDs as needed for pain. We discussed that his symptoms should significantly improve in the next 2 weeks. I recommended that he follow up with me in 2 weeks. He will be able to return to playing soccer once his symptoms have significantly improved.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks. He should continue to elevate his right leg, ice the right ankle, wear an Aircast, use crutches for the next 1-2 days, and walk on it as tolerated. He should take NSAIDs as needed for pain. }}<|eot_id|>
