Bilateral knee pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Sophia Rios is a pleasant 24-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of bilateral knee pain. The onset of the pain was approximately 6 months ago and the patient cannot recall a specific event that caused the pain. The pain is deep achy and localized to the back of the kneecaps. The patient describes hearing creaking in her knees. The patient reports that the pain is worse when getting up from a seated position. She works from home and reports that the pain is worse when she is getting up from her desk. The patient reports that she is able to run 5 to 6 miles per day, but states that she has decreased her mileage to half a mile due to the pain. She denies any swelling or redness in the knees. The patient reports that she has never injured her knees before and denies any history of bone fractures. She has never had any significant knee trauma. She has been running outdoors and on a treadmill. She has been running in Boston and enjoys running near the harbor. She has never broken a bone before. She broke her arm in 6th grade.

MEDICATIONS

Ibuprofen and Aleve.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports bilateral knee pain.
Neurological: Denies numbness or tingling.
Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain.
Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.
HENT: Denies headaches.
Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain.

VITALS

All vital signs are within normal limits.

PHYSICAL EXAM

MSK
- Neurovascular intact distally.
- Strength: 3/5 for abduction of legs bilaterally.
- Remainder of muscle strength of lower extremities is 5/5.
- Gait is normal.
- Examination of the knees: No overlying erythema or ecchymosis appreciated. No warmth of the skin. No effusion. Tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinacular retinaculum. Positive patellar grind test. Negative Lachman anterior and posterior drawer, and McMurray tests bilaterally.

RESULTS

X-ray of the bilateral knees was reviewed today and shows no fractures or osteoarthritis.

ASSESSMENT

Bilateral knee pain.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regard to her current symptoms. I have explained to her that her symptoms are consistent with patellofemoral pain syndrome. I have recommended that she continue taking ibuprofen or anti-inflammatory as needed to help with the pain. I have also recommended that she attend physical therapy for lower extremity exercises to increase her lower extremity strength, mobility, and to correct any incorrect running mechanics.

INSTRUCTIONS

